\chapter{Process Scheduling \& Execution Model}

\section{M:N Threading via Scheduler Activations}

Telix decouples user-level thread scheduling from kernel-level dispatching using $M:N$ Scheduler Activations \footcite{ande92}. The kernel notifies the user-level runtime of blocking events (e.g., page faults, I/O delays) via explicit upcalls, enabling user space to schedule another ready thread without losing CPU allocations.

\section{Fair-Share and Co-Scheduling Mechanics}

Preemptive task selection within a multikernel core domain is managed by an Earliest Eligible Virtual Deadline First (EEVDF) scheduler \footcite{stoi95}. For parallel workloads spanning multiple cores, Telix coordinate core scheduling using matrix gang scheduling techniques \footcite{oust82, feit92} to avoid lock-step waiting across parallel thread boundaries.
